{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC4 verified post-deploy, and the campaign has produced its FIRST DATA\n\nChange 2589 merged and deployed. All five metrics are publishing to `rebar/host`:\n\n    mem_available_percent          1 series\n    mem_used_percent               1 series\n    mem_probe_ok                   1 series\n    container_memory_rss_bytes     4 series\n    container_stats_ok             1 series\n\n**The cardinality fix is confirmed live.** `container_memory_rss_bytes` has exactly FOUR series, and the `service` dimension values are `gerrit`, `mcp`, `review-bot`, `opcert` — four stable service names, not one per deploy. Given I observed three distinct MCP container names in a single session, the unfixed version would already have three series for MCP alone and would grow on every cutover.\n\n## First readings — steady state, no gate running\n\n    gerrit      0.88 GB\n    mcp         1.08 GB\n    review-bot  0.17 GB\n    opcert      0.04 GB\n    -----------------------\n    total      ~2.17 GB of 8 GiB\n\n## The host gauge is already showing something worth knowing\n\n    14:46 PDT  mem_available_percent = 29%\n    14:51 PDT  mem_available_percent = 31%\n    14:56 PDT  mem_available_percent = 60%\n\n`mem_probe_ok = 1.0` across these, so they are MEASURED readings rather than the pessimistic default a failed read publishes — the flag added for exactly this reason is doing its job on its first real use.\n\n**29% available on an 8 GiB box is roughly 2.3 GiB free.** Then something released ~2.3 GiB between 14:51 and 14:56, taking it to 60%. That is the shape of a large consumer exiting — a gate run finishing, or a container cycling.\n\nThis is directly relevant to the OOM: MCP sits at 1.08 GB at rest, and the box was down to ~2.3 GiB free. A gate run that grows the MCP process by ~2 GB against that headroom exhausts the host, which is consistent with the observed `OOMKilled=true / Exit=137` roughly three minutes into a plan-review run.\n\n**It is NOT yet enough to choose a number.** What is still missing for AC5: peak MCP RSS DURING a gate (the `GATE_PEAK_RSS` marker has to fire on a real run), how peak scales with ticket size and fan-out width, and whether the 29% trough is routine or was itself gate-driven. One trough is an observation, not a distribution. The campaign needs to span several gate runs before a `mem_limit` or an instance size is anything but a guess — which was the whole point of measuring first."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788472939935556001,"uuid":"90c59037-53bd-423e-a1fc-899aa424aa2a"}